  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 PM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nIts another beautiful day across southern Indiana and central \nKentucky, with current temperatures in the upper 60s/low 70s and \nmostly clear skies. A broad area of surface high pressure extends \noff to our north, as well as a sturdy upper-level ridge that is \ncentered over the central CONUS. Northwesterly flow aloft continues \nto persist, allowing dry air to remain in place. Daytime mixing has \nallowed RH values to drop into the lower 30% range, with some places \ngetting into the 20s. This trend will continue through at least the \nend of the workweek while northwesterly flow remains. While we are \nable to mix out well during the daytime hours, decent radiational \ncooling will allow overnight temperatures to drop to near or below \nnormal for this time of year. Lows in the upper 30s to low 40s are \nforecast tonight, allowing for patchy frost to develop in low-lying \nrural areas. Additionally, there will be the potential for fog to \ndevelop tonight, though the persistence of dry air over the region \nshould keep this confined to river valleys most prone to fog \ndevelopment.\n\nThis stretch of wonderful weather will continue through the weekend \nand into next week as the broad area of surface high pressure \npersists over the region. The area of surface high pressure will \nfurther build this weekend, extending to the south and east as it \ndoes so. Meanwhile, the upper-level ridge will slowly shift eastward \nover time, with the axis of the ridge moving over the region by \nearly next week. While there may be some subtle wind direction \nshifts as these features shift, the overall forecast remains fairly \nconsistent across the region. Highs through the end of the forecast \nperiod will be in the 70s, with lows in the upper 30s/low 40s \nthrough Saturday, and broadly in the 40s through next Thursday.\n\nOur next chance for appreciable rainfall still remains uncertain, \nwith some spread in solutions showing up in deterministic and \nensemble guidance. Models are picking up on a weak front moving \nthrough the Ohio Valley during the middle parts of next week, though \nthe antecedent dry air over the region could limit the potential for \nrain. LREF guidance only shows a 10-15% chance for rain during the \nWednesday-Thursday timeframe, so chances are likely low with that \nfeature. Chances do increase some (20-30%) beyond the end of the \nforecast period on Halloween, but the uncertainty in model guidance \nright now does not give much confidence in highlighting a solid \npotential for decent rainfall.",
